<script>
  // NoRecord.mesa — a detail screen with no row, and WHY.
  //
  // Not `EmptyState`, which says a LIST has nothing in it. This says a screen
  // that expected one record has none, and the difference between the reasons
  // is the whole component: a person looking at a deleted row, a person whose
  // session ended and a person whose request failed each need a different next
  // move, and telling them apart is not something seven screens should each
  // decide (`FJS-968`).
  //
  // **The reason comes off `error.code` and nowhere else.** Junction's client
  // puts the status there on the HTTP path and on the WS path alike, so it is
  // the one property both transports set. A rejected fetch carries none, which
  // is what makes *no code* mean *the request never got an answer* rather than
  // an unknown status.
  //
  // Three failures this replaces, and only the first was filed:
  //
  //   `apps/[id]` said "App not found — it may have been deleted, or it
  //   belongs to another workspace" for ANY throw in load(). A network blip
  //   read as a deletion. The alert above it said what happened; the HEADING
  //   made a claim about the data, and a heading is what a person and a screen
  //   reader both land on.
  //
  //   Five screens rendered NOTHING when the row was genuinely absent — no
  //   heading, no sentence, no link. `apps/[id]` already carried a comment
  //   saying that exact bug had been fixed there, and it had been fixed there
  //   only.
  //
  //   The same five rendered a failure as an alert with no `<h1>` above it, so
  //   the only statement of what happened was styled as a decoration.
  //
  // **There is no 403 branch and that is measured, not an oversight.** Every
  // model behind a detail screen here reads at 2 and every workspace member
  // grades at least that, so no member is refused; a member looking at another
  // workspace's record is FILTERED by tenancy and arrives as an absence. A
  // refusal would fall through to the failure branch and show its own message,
  // which is honest. The day a screen reads a model gated above its audience,
  // that branch comes back with a test — `example`'s reports screen is the
  // shape it takes.
  import Alert  from '@frontierjs/ui/components/feedback/Alert.mesa'
  import Button from '@frontierjs/ui/components/forms/Button.mesa'

  export let error     = null      // what load() caught, or null for a plain absence
  export let noun      = 'Record'  // 'App', 'Server' — sentence case, singular
  export let backHref  = null
  export let backLabel = 'Go back'
  export let onretry   = null      // a snippet-free callback; omitted → no retry button

  $: lower = noun.toLowerCase()
  $: code  = error?.code ?? error?.status ?? null

  // Absent with no error is the same answer as 404: the read succeeded and
  // there is no such row for this caller. A row policy filtering it out lands
  // here too, which is why the sentence names the workspace.
  $: gone    = !error || code === 404
  $: expired = code === 401
  $: heading = gone    ? `${noun} not found`
             : expired ? 'Signed out'
             :           `Could not load this ${lower}`
</script>

<div class="section-header"><h1>{heading}</h1></div>

{#if gone}
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    It may have been deleted, or it belongs to another workspace.
  </p>
{:else if expired}
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    Your session has ended. <a class="navlink" href="/login/">Sign in</a> and try again.
  </p>
{:else}
  <!-- The message is shown rather than summarised: it is the only thing that
       distinguishes a timeout from a server error, and the person reading it
       is the one who will report it. -->
  <Alert tone="danger" id="screen-error">{error?.message ?? 'The request failed.'}</Alert>
{/if}

<div class="cluster">
  {#if onretry && !gone}
    <Button variant="primary" onclick={onretry}>Try again</Button>
  {/if}
  {#if backHref}
    <Button variant="ghost" href={backHref}>{backLabel}</Button>
  {/if}
</div>
